d here. Let me clarify.\\n\\nWhen you Chi, you take the discarded tile and combine it with two from your hand. So in this case, the user has B4 and B6 in their hand. The discarded B5 is added to form the Chow. So the user's hand would lose one B5 (the one they have) to form the meld? Wait no, when you Chi, you take the discarded tile (B5) and add it to your two tiles (B4 and B6) to form the Chow. So the user's hand would have their existing B4 and B6, and then take the B5 from the discard. So the total tiles in hand would decrease by one (since the B5 is taken from the discard, not from their own hand). Wait, the user's hand already has B5 once. If they take the discarded B5, they would have two B5s. But to form the Chow, they need B4, B5, B6. So they can use one B5 from their hand and the discarded B5? No, wait, when you Chi, you have to have two tiles in your hand that can form a sequence with the discarded tile. So if the user has B4 and B6, and the discarded B5 is available, they can form B4, B5, B6. So the user would discard one of their existing tiles (maybe B5?) but no, when you Chi, you don't discard a tile; you form a meld. Wait, no. When you Chi, you take the discarded tile (B5) and add it to your hand's B4 and B6 to make a Chow. So the user's hand would now have the B4, B5 (discarded), and B6 as a meld. So the user's hand would lose the B4 and B6 from their hand, but gain the B5 from the discard. Wait, no, when you Chi, you are forming a meld using the discarded tile and two from your hand. So the user's hand would have the two tiles (B4 and B6) plus the discarded B5. The total tiles in hand would decrease by one because you're using two of your own tiles and adding the discarded one. Wait, the initial hand has 12 tiles. After forming a meld of three tiles (two from hand, one from discard), the hand size becomes 12 - 2 = 10 tiles. Because you're using two tiles from your hand (B4 and B6) and taking one from the discard (B5). So the meld is formed, and the