ard a tile that's less likely to be needed. T6 is part of the existing chi, so discarding it might be safe. The meld is already there, so the other T6 in hand is redundant. \n\nSo the answer is to play T6.\n", "output": "Evaluation: [Discarding T6 is a safe move as it's part of an existing meld, reducing redundancy and avoiding potential exposure to others needing it for a chi. Other tiles like B7 and B8 are part of potential future chows or pungs, so retaining them is beneficial.]\nReason: [T6 is already part of the [T6, T7, T8] meld, making the second T6 redundant. Discarding it minimizes risk while preserving key tiles (B7, B8) for possible future chows or pungs. The unshown B9 has only one remaining, so retaining B8 could help form a B7-B8-B9 chi if drawn.]\nAnswer: Play T6"}
